t valid action or ordered batch selected by your code. If your code has found a reliable short sequence, prefer batching it in one call.\nYou may call `action(actions)` more than once in one Python snippet if your search or control loop needs it.\nIf you include assistant text before a tool call, keep it short and use it to update the world model. Helpful optional prefixes are `World model:`, `Goal model:`, `Action model:`, `Recent findings:`, `Open questions:`, `Plan:`, and `Cross-level notes:`.\nWhen calling `python`, emit exactly the tool-call format shown elsewhere in this prompt for this model. Use only that format; do not add markdown fences, prose wrappers, or alternate tool-call syntax. Do not quote or place tool-call markup inside explanatory text; when you decide to call the tool, emit the tool call itself.\nIf you use MOUSE, include integer row and col arguments.", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}, {"label": "THINKING", "content": "Interesting! Click 8 at (33, 21) caused game over. The pattern seems to be:\n- 7 green blocks can be clicked successfully (the top row of 7)\n- The 8th click (first block of the second row) causes game over\nBut in the first successful attempt, I was able to click more than 7 blocks. Let me think about what was different...\nIn the first successful attempt:\n1. I clicked 3 purple objects\n2. Clicked 1 green block (27, 15) - worked\n3. Clicked 13 remaining green blocks in a batch - the board scrolled/changed\n4. Clicked 5 more green blocks - level changed\nIn the second attempt:\n1. I clicked 6 purple objects (in the scrolled view)\n2. Clicked 6 green blocks in a batch - game over\nIn the third attempt:\n1. I clicked 3 purple objects\n2. Clicked 1 green block - worked\n3. Clicked 6 more green blocks (top row) - worked\n4. Clicked 1 more (33, 15) - worked\n5. Clicked (33, 21) - game over\nSo the pattern is: after clicking purple objects, I can click 7 green blocks (the top row), but clicking the 8th one (first of second row) ca